        <div id="healthy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is Gladiator Protein Healthy? Sweetener, Soy and Lactose
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gladiator protein is a reasonable choice for most healthy adults: it is high in protein, low in sugar at 3 g per serving and low in fat. The trade-offs are an artificial sweetener, two gums, a proprietary blend and a big vitamin dose. None of those is a red flag on its own, but each matters to a different kind of buyer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does Gladiator protein contain soy, egg or lactose?</h3>

          <p>
            The tub label says "Contains: Egg, milk, soy" for all three flavors. Smoothie King's website lists only egg and milk as allergens, so the printed label is the one to trust, and anyone with a soy allergy should avoid it until the brand says otherwise. Egg comes from the egg white solids, and milk from the whey and casein. The label does not claim to be lactose-free. Whey isolate and hydrolysate are naturally low in lactose and the enzyme blend includes lactase, but micellar casein still carries some, so people with marked lactose intolerance may do better with a pure isolate.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sucralose, gums and cholesterol</h3>

          <p>
            Sucralose is the only sweetener. It is approved by the FDA and widely used, though some people notice an aftertaste or prefer to avoid it; if so, Klean Isolate's unflavored version has no sweetener at all. Xanthan and guar gum thicken the shake and are well tolerated by most people, though large amounts can cause gas in sensitive guts. Each serving also carries 35 mg of cholesterol from the egg and dairy, which is about 12 percent of the daily value.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A lot of vitamins in one scoop</h3>

          <p>
            One serving supplies 300 percent of the daily value for vitamin C and vitamin B12, 50 percent for vitamin A, and 50 percent for iron at 9 mg. If you also take a multivitamin, or drink a Gladiator smoothie on the same day, you can double up. Extra iron in particular is not ideal for most men and post-menopausal women, who rarely need supplemental iron unless a doctor has said so.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have kidney disease, are pregnant or breastfeeding, take medication, or are an older adult with a medical condition, talk to your doctor or a registered dietitian before adding a 45 g protein serving every day. The label itself tells pregnant or nursing women to consult a healthcare professional.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Do Buyers Say About Gladiator Taste and Mixing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            There is very little published buyer feedback on the Gladiator tub itself. Because it is not sold on Amazon or other big online stores, it has no large pool of verified reviews, and most of what you will find online is about the Gladiator smoothie made in the shop. We have not tasted it, and we would rather say that than invent a verdict.
          </p>

          <p>
            What we can say from the label is how it is built to taste and mix. All three flavors are sweetened with sucralose and thickened with xanthan and guar gum, the same approach used by most mainstream whey powders, so expect a sweet, fairly thick shake rather than a thin, juice-like one. Chocolate uses alkalized cocoa, and strawberry is colored with beet powder. The casein and gums make it better suited to a blender with ice and fruit, the way Smoothie King uses it, than to a quick shake in a bottle with water.
          </p>

          <p>
            The common praise for the in-store Gladiator smoothie, as buyers describe it in reviews and on social media, is that it tastes good for a 45 g protein drink and keeps them full. The common complaints are the price of the smoothie and, for the plain versions, that it tastes like a protein shake rather than a fruit smoothie. Since the tub is the same blend, those are fair expectations for home use too.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buyers who want a flavor guide built on wider review data will find more options in our <a href="/best-tasting-protein-powder/">best tasting protein powder</a> roundup, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-ninja-creami/">Ninja Creami protein powder</a> guide covers casein-heavy powders like this one that thicken well when frozen.
          </p>
