        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Gladiator Protein, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gladiator protein is worth buying for Smoothie King regulars who want the same protein at home, and for tested athletes who like a high-dose, NSF-certified shake. It is worth skipping for anyone shopping on price, anyone avoiding soy or sucralose, and anyone who would rather order online than visit a store.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy Gladiator if</h3>

          <p>
            You already drink Gladiator smoothies and want to cut the cost of a daily habit; your nearest store prices the tub near $44 to $50; you want one big 45 g serving after training rather than two smaller scoops; or you compete in a sport that tests for banned substances and want the NSF Certified for Sport mark. The mix of fast whey and slower casein also makes it a sensible choice for a meal-replacement-style shake or a pre-bed serving, and its low carb count suits people following a lower-carb plan or the higher-protein eating that often goes with <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-glp-1/">GLP-1 medication</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Skip Gladiator if</h3>

          <p>
            Your store charges $55 or more, at which point Muscle Milk Pro Series or a mainstream whey is cheaper per gram; you have a soy or egg allergy; you want a fully transparent label with an amount for each protein source; or you need a dairy-free or vegan protein. Light eaters who only want 20 to 25 g at a time can use one scoop, but then they are paying a premium for a dose they could get from almost any whey. And if you never visit a Smoothie King, the hassle of buying in person is reason enough to choose an Amazon option.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you choose, the decision usually comes down to three numbers: protein per serving, cost per 25 g and testing status. Gladiator scores well on the first and third and only average on the second. That makes it a good product with a store-dependent price, which is why we say check your local price before you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

