                  The tub label says it contains egg, milk and soy, even though Smoothie King's website lists only egg and milk. It is not labeled lactose-free. Whey isolate and hydrolysate are low in lactose and the enzyme blend includes lactase, but the micellar casein still carries some, so very lactose-sensitive buyers may prefer a pure whey isolate.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which Gladiator flavor is best?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  There is too little buyer feedback on the tubs to rank the flavors reliably, and we have not tasted them. Chocolate has the most fiber and potassium on the label, while vanilla is the most flexible for blending with fruit, which is how Smoothie King uses it. Strawberry is colored with beet powder. All three share the same 45 g protein serving.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Where can I buy Gladiator protein powder, and what are the complaints?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Tubs are sold at Smoothie King stores and through each location's online ordering; we found no Amazon listing. The main complaints are price, since store prices we found ran from $43.99 to $79.99 per 2 lb tub, and the inconvenience of buying in person. Buyers of the in-store smoothie mostly praise its taste and how filling it is.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

